WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.080
The other library I just want to do a quick show and tell

00:02.080 --> 00:05.400
demo on is, if you haven't heard of this, this library

00:05.400 --> 00:08.640
ViewUse is a phenomenal library.

00:08.640 --> 00:12.600
It is a bunch of wrappers around basically browser APIs.

00:12.600 --> 00:15.480
So things that you might commonly want to do say, I

00:15.480 --> 00:18.160
don't know, you want to be able to do something with a clip

00:18.160 --> 00:19.720
board?

00:19.720 --> 00:20.640
This should look familiar.

00:20.640 --> 00:22.920
It says, oh, this is a use clipboard function that we

00:22.920 --> 00:24.040
can import.

00:24.040 --> 00:27.200
And when you import it, it'll actually basically copy over

00:27.200 --> 00:32.480
whatever it is as a v-model and whatnot, and it just works.

00:32.480 --> 00:36.360
And so to show you just as a show and tell on this, something

00:36.360 --> 00:39.400
that we often want to do when it comes to apps is use local

00:39.400 --> 00:40.200
storage.

00:40.200 --> 00:42.720
And if you've never done that from scratch, it's a pretty good

00:42.720 --> 00:46.080
API to use, but there is a bit to do as far as manually

00:46.080 --> 00:48.840
configuring that in addition to managing reactive state.

00:48.840 --> 00:51.840
So one of my favorite functions is the use storage here with

00:51.840 --> 00:52.320
Pinnier.

00:52.320 --> 00:56.480
So if we look at Pinnier use storage with ViewUse, you can

00:56.480 --> 01:00.000
see that ViewUse.

01:00.000 --> 01:00.880
OK, here we go.

01:00.880 --> 01:03.480
This is a blog post that actually shows how it would work.

01:03.480 --> 01:06.400
So basically, if I scroll down here, you'll notice that

01:06.400 --> 01:09.240
inside of this to-do list, you have this one line right here

01:09.240 --> 01:11.560
that allows you to import from use storage.

01:11.560 --> 01:13.480
And what it does is basically creates that local storage

01:13.480 --> 01:16.760
key, and then every time it's being updated, when Pinnier

01:16.760 --> 01:20.000
loads, it will know with ViewUse's help to check the local

01:20.000 --> 01:21.760
storage first to see if there's new data.

01:21.760 --> 01:23.640
If there's already existing data, it'll actually start from

01:23.640 --> 01:27.040
the local storage and not from this initialized value.

01:27.040 --> 01:29.480
And so what you end up getting is this really elegant

01:29.480 --> 01:33.320
coming together of your ViewUse functionality with your

01:33.320 --> 01:35.720
reactive data inside of your ViewApp.

01:35.720 --> 01:37.520
And so other ones that are really fun that you might

01:37.520 --> 01:40.640
want to check out as well is the color preference, like

01:40.640 --> 01:41.880
color mode.

01:41.880 --> 01:44.680
And so what you see here is that what this is doing is that

01:44.680 --> 01:47.240
it's basically a reactive property that allows you to

01:47.240 --> 01:51.520
easily toggle the state of the theme of your app.

01:52.280 --> 01:54.200
There's so much stuff in here because basically what they've

01:54.200 --> 01:56.840
done is taken a bunch of browser APIs and made it really

01:56.840 --> 01:59.120
easy to integrate inside of your ViewApp.

01:59.120 --> 02:03.600
So definitely highly recommended as one to check out.

02:03.600 --> 02:06.520
The other final thing that I want to shout out is regarding

02:06.520 --> 02:07.680
transitions in View.

02:10.080 --> 02:12.760
So as we know, creating good user experiences, that's a

02:12.760 --> 02:15.680
really important part of making things

02:15.680 --> 02:17.320
easier for users to follow.

02:17.320 --> 02:20.720
And so we don't have time to go into detail on this, but

02:20.720 --> 02:22.760
basically there are built-in components called

02:22.760 --> 02:25.640
transition and transition group that help you manage how

02:25.640 --> 02:27.720
things are being attached as they exit.

02:27.720 --> 02:29.800
So the most common example that I'll show right now, because

02:29.800 --> 02:33.120
you can check out the docs, the one that I love to show is

02:33.120 --> 02:36.120
the one for transition group, because this is the one that

02:36.120 --> 02:37.760
I think we do a lot.

02:37.760 --> 02:45.360
So if I go down into transition, transition group, a lot

02:45.360 --> 02:47.320
of us render lists.

02:47.320 --> 02:51.160
Rather than have them blink in like this, you can also have

02:51.160 --> 02:53.520
them move in a way that's even smoother.

02:53.520 --> 02:57.480
You see here, this actually allows you to shuffle stuff.

02:57.480 --> 02:59.360
And all that is being done, how?

02:59.360 --> 03:02.920
It's being done by wrapping basically your component

03:02.920 --> 03:04.760
inside of this transition group.

03:04.760 --> 03:06.960
There's a little bit of CSS that happens there, but I'll

03:06.960 --> 03:09.280
leave you to check out the docs to get inspiration from

03:09.280 --> 03:09.800
that.

03:09.800 --> 03:12.640
But just know that this is built into the library so that

03:12.640 --> 03:14.600
if you want to try playing around with it, making the

03:14.600 --> 03:16.840
users easier to track things.

03:16.880 --> 03:18.960
This is also where the keys are very important, because as

03:18.960 --> 03:21.600
you can see, we're doing things like shuffling here.

03:21.600 --> 03:24.960
View needs to be able to know which element it is, where it

03:24.960 --> 03:27.440
needs to go as it's moving stuff around.

03:27.440 --> 03:32.600
So this is another bit that if you all are developing for

03:32.600 --> 03:35.040
your apps, definitely check out the transition group and

03:35.040 --> 03:37.600
transition for ways to enhance your user experience.

